      "crop_slug": "cilantro-coriander",
      "field_path": "regions.pnw.region_notes_beginner",
      "our_prose": "In the maritime Pacific Northwest, cilantro gets a real break most of the country does not: our cool, mild summer rarely gets hot enough to bolt it the way a hot inland summer would. Direct sow as soon as the soil can be worked in late winter or early spring, since cilantro takes a light frost, and sow a little every 2 to 3 weeks for a steady supply. Ease off new sowings around midsummer when the days are longest, then sow again in late summer for a fall crop that keeps going right up to the first frost, which is often cilantro's best season here.",
      "run_normalized": "in late winter or early spring",
      "run_in_our_prose": "in late winter or early spring",
      "run_words": 6,
