    {
      "crop_slug": "cilantro-coriander",
      "field_path": "regions.utah_dixie.region_notes_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "Cilantro is not one of USU Extension's Table 1 vegetable rows, so this spring window follows the Group A (Hardy) February 15 date given to St. George's other cold-hardy, quick-germinating spring crops. Leaves are ready in about 60 days; cilantro's own heat threshold runs around 75°F, one of the lowest of this family's herbs, so the plant bolts to coriander seed early once the season warms. St. George's summers routinely reach 100°F in June and July, per USU Extension's Washington County frost and elevation data, so leaf sowing pauses through that stretch. A second direct sowing in early August, once the worst heat has broken, follows USU's general fall-gardening guidance for restarting cool-season crops (Fall Gardening in the St. George Area); the fall stand crops from late September up to just ahead of the average November 1 first frost, with less pressure to bolt as days shorten.",
